---
import Hero from "../components/layout/hero/Hero.astro";
import Heading from "../components/ui/Heading.astro";
import Link from "../components/ui/Link.astro";
import P from "../components/ui/P.astro";
import Main from "../layouts/Main.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";
import SocialMediaLinks from "../components/feature/social-media-links/SocialMediaLinks.astro";
import Values from "../components/feature/values/Values.astro";
---

<Main>
  <Hero slot="hero" />
  <div slot="content">
    <P>
      Hello 👋... I am Conrad, a human living in
      <Link href="https://www.southafrica.net/" target="_blank"
        >South Africa.
      </Link> I guess if you read this, you would like to know a little about me,
      so here it is…
    </P>
    <Heading>I have a career</Heading>
    <P>
      Professionally, I have worn many hats. I began as a Web Designer and
      dabbled in some Graphic Design, predominantly creating corporate
      identities. Midway into my journey, I transitioned to Frontend Engineering
      and Development. I transitioned to the role of Engineering Manager over
      the last two years. I've also unofficially worn the hats of Product
      Manager, Product Designer and UX researcher, albeit just briefly 🤏.
    </P>
    <P>
      As a <strong>Software Engineer</strong>, I take a pragmatic approach to
      designing and building software. I always try to balance shipping the best
      we have today with an execution plan to evolve and iterate on later. I've
      been a tech lead on big initiatives and find great joy in being involved
      in projects end-to-end. I have built SaaS software for over a decade. My
      main philosophy when building SaaS products is to get users to play with
      it as early as possible. If no one is using it, you won't know which parts
      of your product are pain points and which parts are joys. Also, do
      behavioural and product analytics and continuous discovery to make better
      decisions. I've always leaned more towards the Frontend part of software
      engineering, because I like design. I geek out on the little UI
      interaction that seems negligible but makes the experience feel polished.
      Most importantly, what I build should satisfy the user's needs and make
      the desired jobs-to-be-done easier, not harder.
    </P>
    <P>
      As an <strong>Engineering Manager</strong>, I have overseen many different
      teams, and sometimes multiple teams at the same time. My outlook on
      managing is to trust by default. The engineers I manage were hired because
      they are good at their jobs. I have a bias towards trusting them and only
      address the outliers. It is important to always have fun. Play games
      (GeoGuessr, Gartic Phone, take your pick), get the team invested in the
      same goals and celebrate together.
    </P>
    <P>
      I am constantly learning where the sweet spot is between my technical
      involvement, active contribution and taking a high-level view on current
      and future initiatives. Many people write about this balance, but the
      reality is much harder to get just right 🫠. My own conclusion on this is
      that each phase in the Product lifecycle requires a different skillset to
      manage, and when it does, don't feel bad about temporarily neglecting the
      others a bit, because the pendulum will swing and those will come to the
      foreground inevitably.
    </P>
    <Heading>My core values</Heading>
    <P
      >I've worked at a company called Hotjar, which had good core values
      guiding their decision-making on all levels of the organisation. This was
      the best example of Core Values done right that I've ever experienced.
      Unfortunately, most companies do not really respect their Core Values at
      an organisational level, creating a disconnected and frustrating
      experience.
    </P>
    <P>
      After Hotjar, I was confronted by this disconnect between Company's Core
      Values and contrasting decision-making. As a response, I created my own,
      personal, set of Core Values in which I function every day. I periodically
      revisit these values, making sure I still adhere to them and that they are
      still relevant.
    </P>
    <Values />
    <Heading>I have a personal life too, you know</Heading>
    <P
      >In my personal life, I am a successful family man who loves his wife of
      12 years and has 3 beautiful children. I don't claim to be perfect and am
      constantly growing as a person. But I enjoy life, am kind where I can be
      and ask myself the hard questions when I find it difficult. I believe in
      the power of reflection and soul searching.
    </P>
    <P>
      Also, I love swimming. Which is what inspired the design of this website
      🏊🏻.
    </P>
    <Heading>More</Heading>
    <P> You can find me in some other places of the internet too: </P>
    <SocialMediaLinks class="flex flex-col gap-2" />
  </div>
</Main>
